Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
1. Using GridBagLayout
GridBagLayout is a flexible layout manager that arranges components in rows and columns.
- It uses constraints to control the position and size of each component.
- weightx and weighty decide how extra space is distributed.
- The results area can use weightx = 1 and weighty = 1 to expand.
- Search and bottom controls can keep their preferred sizes using suitable fill and weight settings.
2. Using absolute positioning
Absolute positioning uses:
setLayout(null);

searchField.setBounds(20, 50, 200, 30);
searchButton.setBounds(230, 50, 100, 30);


- Every component receives fixed x, y, width and height values.
- Components do not automatically adjust when the window is resized.
- Manual resize calculations are required.
- It can cause overlapping or hidden components on different screen sizes.
3. Comparison
Feature	Nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout	GridBagLayout	Absolute positioning
Resizing	Automatically adjusts centre	Adjusts using constraints	Requires manual adjustment
Design	Simple	More complex	Simple initially
Flexibility	Good	Very high	Low
Layout calculation	Generally O(n)	Generally O(n)	No automatic layout calculation
Maintenance	Easy	Moderate	Difficult
Space	O(c)	O(c)	O(c)

Here, c represents the number of GUI components.
4. Layout computation time
- BorderLayout and FlowLayout: They arrange components based on their preferred sizes and available space. Layout computation is generally O(n) for n components.
- GridBagLayout: It processes component constraints and calculates positions and sizes. Its layout computation is generally O(n) for n components, with additional constraint-processing overhead.
- Absolute positioning: It does not perform automatic layout calculations. Assigning bounds to n components takes O(n) time. If manual resizing is implemented, the resize handler must also update the component bounds.
5. Space complexity
- Nested layout design: O(c) for components and O(p) for p panels.
- GridBagLayout: O(c) for components and their layout constraints.
- Absolute positioning: O(c) for components and their stored bounds.
Note: Layout manager objects and container objects also require memory. The exact memory usage depends on the implementation and number of components.
Final Result
The Campus Library Search Screen was designed and implemented using Java Swing. Nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout managers organise the title, search controls, results area, navigation buttons and status message. During resizing, the results area expands or shrinks while the other controls maintain their preferred sizes. The design is easier to maintain and more flexible than absolute positioning.
